---
title: Naan (Peshwari / Garlic and Coriander)
yield: 6%naan
course: Bread
prep time: 30m
cook time: 15m
locale: en
difficulty: Medium
cuisine: Indian
---

= Naan Dough =

Mix the dry dough ingredients (@self raising flour{325%g}, @?nigella seeds{2%tbsp}, @icing sugar{1%tbsp}, @baking powder{1%tsp}, and @salt{0.75%tsp}) in a large bowl. Add the @flavorless oil{1%tbsp}, @full-fat yoghurt{2%tbsp}, and @warm water{150%ml}. Mix to create a very sticky dough; do not add extra flour. Cover and leave to stand for ~{30%minutes} at room temperature (or in the fridge for up to ~{24%hours}).

= Peshwari Filling =

To make the peshwari filling: mix all the ingredients (@sultanas{2%tbsp}(chopped), @cream{4%tbsp}, @coconut milk powder{110%g}, @ground almonds{2%tbsp}, @ground pistachios{2%tbsp}, @golden syrup{2%tbsp}, and @ground cardamom{4%pods}) in a small bowl to form a sticky paste.

> This may be prepared up to 48 hours in advance.

= Coriander-Garlic Butter =

To make the coriander-garlic butter: Melt the @salted butter (or ghee){125%g} in a small saucepan. Add the @garlic{3%cloves}(crushed and chopped) and cook for 90 seconds. Remove from the heat and add the @coriander{2%tbsp}(chopped).

= Cook Naan =

Preheat a #pizza stone{} to 300°C for at least ~{30%minutes}.

Divide the dough into 6 equal portions.

For peshwari naan: Press each piece into a small circle, and place a portion of the stuffing on each. Lift the sides up and pinch together to fully enclose the filling. Pat into a round disc.

On a lightly floured surface roll each piece into a teardrop shape.

Cook in batches on the pizza stone until brown patches appear, but still soft, approximately ~{5%minutes}.

For coriander-garlic naan: Brush with the butter.